# The Enterarchon Psychology: The Enteric Brain Is Primary

**Christopher Macachor**

**DOI:** [10.5281/zenodo.21849213](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21849213)

---

## Abstract

The central nervous system has been erroneously privileged as the seat of consciousness, identity, and governance. This paper advances the Enterarchon Hypothesis: the enteric nervous system (ENS) is not a "second brain" subordinate to cortical control, but the primary neural architecture from which all higher-order cognition emerges. Drawing on comparative evidence across metazoans—from cnidarians to mammals—we demonstrate that gut-embedded circuits arose first, scaffolded later centralization, and remain the sovereign regulator of organismal state. We introduce the concept of the microbial vector dampener: the gut microbiome functions as a perceptual filter that modulates environmental input into coherent neural signal. In individuals where this dampener is attenuated, we observe a condition of unfiltered enteric reception—wherein the organism perceives environmental density gradients without the buffering layer that typically protects the central nervous system from overwhelm. The intestinal barrier is reconceptualized not merely as a physical seal but as a coherence aperture: when it fails, the organism undergoes a decoherence cascade that predicts mortality across species. We argue that psychology must be re-founded on enteric principles: emotion, intuition, and social cognition are not cortical luxuries but visceral necessities. The brain did not evolve to rule the gut. The gut evolved, and the brain followed.

**Keywords:** enteric nervous system, Enterarchon, microbiome, empathy, intestinal barrier, visceral brain, neurogastroenterology, embodied cognition

---

## I. The Braincentric Error

Western psychology, neuroscience, and medicine share a foundational error: they locate the self in the head. The brain is treated as the executive, the decision-maker, the sovereign. The gut is relegated to digestion—a plumbing problem, a peripheral concern, a source of embarrassment between meals.

This is the braincentric error, and it has structured everything from psychiatric diagnosis to artificial intelligence research. We speak of "gut feelings" as metaphors, of "butterflies in the stomach" as poetic license, of intestinal distress as psychosomatic—literally, of the mind-body—as if the soma were secondary to the psyche.

Recent comparative evidence demands a radical inversion. Silva, Lemoine, Rera, and Hadjieconomou (2026) propose the Enterarchon—the ancestral intestinal tube that emerged via epithelial invagination in early metazoans—as the primordial locus of physiological and neural control. In cnidarians (*Hydra*), placozoans, and ctenophores, gut-associated circuits coordinate feeding, peristalsis, immune defense, and metabolic state before synaptic neurotransmission evolves. The ENS is not an afterthought. It is the scaffold upon which centralized nervous systems later arose (Moroz & Kohn, 2021; Jekely, 2021).

This paper argues that the Enterarchon is not merely an evolutionary curiosity. It is the template for psychology itself. Cognition, emotion, social behavior, and perception do not flow downward from cortex to gut. They bubble upward from gut to cortex—or, more precisely, they emerge from the bidirectional interface where the ancient visceral brain meets the later-evolved sensorimotor brain. The cortex does not govern the gut. The gut permits the cortex to exist.

