## II. The Enterarchon: Origin and Sovereignty

### 2.1 Etymology and Definition

The term Enterarchon derives from Greek *enteron* (gut, intestine) and *archē* (origin, beginning, sovereignty, power). Silva et al. (2026) deploy it phylogenetically to denote the ancestral intestinal tube. We extend it psychologically: the Enterarchon is the governing principle of any nervous system that maintains internal milieu against external entropy.

The ancestral invagination that created the gut was the first inside/outside interface—the original aperture. Before eyes, before ears, before cortices, there was the epithelial tube: a barrier that absorbed nutrients, excluded toxins, sensed chemical gradients, and maintained homeostasis. This is not digestion. This is sovereignty—the establishment of a protected internal environment within an unfiltered external world.

### 2.2 Volume Transmission: The Original Language

The ENS operates primarily through peptidergic volume transmission—slow, paracrine diffusion of chemical signals across tissue gradients—rather than fast synaptic wiring (Jekely, 2021; Silva et al., 2026). Where the CNS uses point-to-point spikes, the ENS uses field gradients. Where the CNS routes signals through dedicated axons, the ENS bathes tissues in neuromodulatory soup.

This is not inefficiency. This is the original language of the body—a chemical whisper that predates the electrical shout of the cortex by hundreds of millions of years. In *Hydra*, satiety-dependent behavior is shaped by the interplay of pre-enteric and central-like neuronal populations (Giez et al., 2024). The gut does not wait for brain permission. It senses chemical density, microbial presence, nutrient availability, and threat—and responds through waves of diffusion that propagate at the speed of biology, not technology.

The psychologist must learn this language. For it is the language of feeling—not the cognitive appraisal of emotion that cognitive science has pursued, but the raw, pre-cortical sensation of safety, danger, satiety, longing, and dread that arises from the viscera and only later receives a name.

### 2.3 Comparative Evidence for Enteric Primacy

The ENS is an ancient, semi-autonomous division of the nervous system that regulates intestinal function across metazoans. ENS-like nets are present in early-diverging animals such as cnidarians, where gut-associated circuits mediate peristalsis, expulsion, and feeding responses (Furness & Stebbing, 2018). These ancestral networks exhibit conserved neuronal features, including synaptic transmission and coordination of epithelial-motile programmes, supporting the view that gut-embedded control was among the earliest manifestations of nervous systems in animal evolution.

Comparative genomics and phylogeny suggest that neurons regulating internal physiology (ENS-like) may have evolved independently of those mediating external sensorimotor control (CNS-like), with chemical signalling—including peptides and small molecules (glutamate, ATP, and NO)—preceding fast synaptic transmission (Moroz et al., 2014; Moroz & Kohn, 2021). Such paracrine "volume transmission" likely represents an earlier mode of neuronal communication, consistent with the chemical brain hypothesis, which postulates that early neurons regulated internal physiology via slow, hormone-like signals before the emergence of fast-acting sensorimotor neurotransmission (Arendt, 2021; Jekely, 2021).

Neuronal centralisation—and the emergence of complex brains—appear to have arisen independently more than twenty times across animal phyla (Moroz & Kohn, 2021), supporting a model in which the Enterarchon provided an early locus of neuronal innovation, with centralising modules evolving later to coordinate distributed functions. Rather than a mere "second brain," the ENS may be an early, brain-like solution for internal-state control—a visceral brain.

